<section>
  <h2>Definition</h2>
  <p>A neocloud is a cloud company built around GPU compute for AI work. It rents
  clusters, bare-metal servers or inference by the hour or on a contract. It does not
  try to be a general-purpose cloud with hundreds of other services.</p>
  <p>The label is loose. This site sorts providers into
  <a href="/providers/category/cat-neo/">neoclouds</a>,
  <a href="/providers/category/cat-eu/">European and regional clouds</a>,
  <a href="/providers/category/cat-market/">marketplaces</a>,
  <a href="/providers/category/cat-inference/">inference platforms</a> and
  <a href="/providers/category/cat-landlord/">landlords</a>.
  The <a href="/vocabulary/">vocabulary</a> explains the terms.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Neocloud, hyperscaler or reseller</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Neocloud:</strong> runs GPU clusters as its main product. Example row:
      <a href="/providers/coreweave/">CoreWeave</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Hyperscaler or general cloud:</strong> GPUs are one product among many.
      Example row: <a href="/providers/ovhcloud/">OVHcloud</a>, which this site describes as a
      general-purpose cloud more than a neocloud.</li>
    <li><strong>Marketplace or reseller:</strong> brokers GPUs that other hosts own.
      Example row: <a href="/providers/vast-ai/">Vast.ai</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Landlord:</strong> owns the building and the power, and rents space to
      tenants who bring their own machines. Example rows:
      <a href="/providers/merlin-edged/">Merlin Edged</a> and
      <a href="/providers/applied-digital/">Applied Digital</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>One company can be more than one of these. Check each product, not just the name.</p>
</section>

<section id="compared">
  <h2>Neocloud vs hyperscaler</h2>
  <p>A hyperscaler is a general cloud where GPUs are one product among many. A neocloud sells
  GPU compute as the main product. The two also meet as landlord and tenant: the hyperscalers
  lease whole buildings too.</p>
