      {% include "partials/source-line.njk" %}</li>
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p>A sovereign label is a claim about control. It is not a certificate. Ask who owns and
  operates the site and which law applies to the contract.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Six questions to ask</h2>
  <p>Ask the provider. Get each answer in writing. If it will not answer, treat that as an answer.</p>
  <ol class="checklist">
    <li class="check-item"><strong>Who operates it?</strong> Who runs the machines day to day:
      the company you pay, or a partner? Each provider page has an operating-model line.
      It says <em>unknown</em> until a source states it. See the
      <a href="/providers/">provider index</a>.</li>
    <li class="check-item"><strong>Who owns the GPUs?</strong> The company that sells you
      the service may own the GPUs, lease them, or pass on capacity from someone else. A
      marketplace such as <a href="/providers/vast-ai/">Vast.ai</a> lists hardware owned by
      other hosts.</li>
    <li class="check-item"><strong>Where is the facility?</strong> Ask for the site and who
      owns the building. Some providers rent their halls: the
      <a href="/providers/coreweave/">CoreWeave</a> row notes European sites leased from
      <a href="/providers/merlin-edged/">Merlin Edged</a>. See also the
      <a href="/leasing/">leasing</a> page.</li>
    <li class="check-item"><strong>What does a certificate not prove?</strong> A certificate
      covers a scope: one site, one product, one date. It does not say the facility you use is
      in that scope. Ask which site the certificate names. See the
      <a href="/exams/">exams</a> and <a href="/courses/">courses</a> pages for how
      vendor credentials are treated here.</li>
    <li class="check-item"><strong>Is availability in writing?</strong> A web page that says
      &ldquo;high availability&rdquo; is marketing. Look for an uptime commitment in the
      contract or service terms, and what you get if it is missed.</li>
    <li class="check-item"><strong>What is the minimum term?</strong> Ask for the shortest
      commitment, the notice period and what happens to your data on exit.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

